# The local knowledge-graph working directory. Every `crib index` rewrites thousands of files here,
# which buried real code changes under ~4,700 generated files in review and made `git status`
# unusable. It is all reproducible from source with `crib index .`, so it is build output, not
# source — the same reasoning that already excluded `.crib/index/`.
#
# `.crib/*` (not `.crib/`) with a negation, because git will NOT descend into an excluded DIRECTORY:
# `.crib/` + `!.crib/memory/` silently keeps memory excluded. Excluding the directory's CONTENTS
# instead leaves `.crib/memory/` re-includable.
#
# `.crib/memory/` is the one part that is NOT build output: it is the committed, team-shared memory
# ledger — the product's central promise, and the path `.gitattributes` declares the `kcrib-memory`
# merge driver for. Ignoring it wholesale would have made team memory impossible to share, which is
# the opposite of what this repo exists to do.
#
# `.crib/bins/` is re-included the same way and for the same reason: it is durable work-product
# knowledge owned by the repository (measurements, open decisions, corrections), not build output.
# Its contract lives in `.crib/bins/README.md`. Note that tracking it puts it in scope for
# `scripts/credential-check.mjs`, which lists files with `git ls-files` — so re-run that gate AFTER
# `git add`, since a PASS taken before staging describes the old tree.
.crib/*
!.crib/memory/
!.crib/bins/

# Codex tool state — machine-local, same reasoning.
.codex/

# playwright browser-suite output (WP6 slice D)
test-results/
playwright-report/

# `node scripts/release-evidence.mjs` writes its diagnostic manifest to the repo root by default
# (release.yml overrides --out per-cell before uploading it as an artifact). Running the gate
# locally must not dirty the checkout it certifies (WP0's evidence-outside-tracked-source rule).
release-evidence.json

# `node scripts/sbom.mjs` writes the CycloneDX SBOM to the repo root by default (release-verify
# generates it before the evidence manifest; CI uploads it as an artifact). Same rule as
# release-evidence.json: the artifact is OUTPUT, and running the gate locally must not dirty the
# checkout it certifies. The artifact is what gets published; this file is only where it lands.
sbom.cdx.json

# `crib memory export` output — a generated VIEW of the ledger, never a source of it.
# Committing it would create exactly the second source of truth the one-way design avoids: the file
# would age against `.crib/memory/` and a reader could not tell which was current. Regenerate with
# `crib memory export --out MEMORY.md` whenever you want a readable snapshot.
MEMORY.md
